A||||5||GT Bastard||Loss of power through the drivetrain... Does anybody have exact figures?||||4||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||01-22-2001||11:31 PM||||Does anyone know exactly what kind of horsepower and torque is lost throught the fiero transmission?  I've heard before that F-Bodies typically lose 20% to the rear wheels through and automatic and manuals lost only 15% to the wheels... I would assume that a Fiero loses less due to the fact that it doesn't use a driveshaft, BUT... When 1FST2M6 dyno'd my car(his old SE) for the first time, he said it made just under 100hp and 120ish lbs of torque at the wheels through it's manual tranny.  And his automatic GT dyno'd similarly before he modded it...  So we're looking at a 40% power loss through the drivetrain????  That doesn't sound correct.  But niether my SE nor his GT is a slouch by any means.  And I've seen the same kind of results from other near-stock 2.8's. Is there something I'm missing here?   ||||reg||4
Z||000001||Vader||01-22-2001||11:37 PM||||GT,<P>Whatever the loss percentage might be, it's a lot less with synthetic engine oil in the transaxle.  The down side is that the synchros don't seem to work as well with the synthetic, so expect some slower downshifts.<P>Incidentally, my TA makes 312 RWHP, so I think the "20%" loss estimate might be a little high.  I use synthetics in that as well.<BR>------------------<BR>"<I>Make Me Bad</I>"<p>[This message has been edited by Vader (edited 01-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-23-2001||12:05 AM||||it depends on the gear set. in an auto it also depends if the TC is locked or not.<P>In an auto you actually gain torque under certain conditions because of the TC torque multiplier effect.<P>If the TC is locked the auto is no worse than the stick when cruising. At this point it's totally up to the tranny gearing and the final drive set. All depends on the tranny in question.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||01-23-2001||09:25 AM||||I asked a similar question on the MAFOA list after a guy was done with a V8 install, he used the ZZ4 and his Hp to the wheels was 260 and torque was 340. (www.nytmare.org/fiero)<P>The GM performance place lists the ZZ4 as 385 Hp or something like that.  His reply was that the ZZ4 tested by the GM performance place had some Corvette parts on it that made more power.  Still though it seems Fiero's lose more in the drivetrain then other cars, when you would think it would lose less.  Never did get a straight answer on the mailing list.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000004||1FST2M6||01-23-2001||09:34 AM||||autos will have more torque down low and less tq and hp when the viscus coupling looses it effectiveness (torque converter) 10-15% on stick and 20-25% on auto. <P><BR>Dr./Prof. Skinner (internal combustion lab at Ga. Tech) ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||01-23-2001||10:36 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>it depends on the gear set. in an auto it also depends if the TC is locked or not.<P>In an auto you actually gain torque under certain conditions because of the TC torque multiplier effect.<P>If the TC is locked the auto is no worse than the stick when cruising. At this point it's totally up to the tranny gearing and the final drive set. All depends on the tranny in question.<P>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-23-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>An auto will ALWAYS have more loss than a manual because the pump is always turning.<P>Percentage loss is very difficult to quantify. Some losses are fixed, some losses depend on speed, some losses depend on torque, and some losses depend on both. Overall, a transverse manual drivetrain will have the lowest loss because (at least in Getrags) there are two spur gear transfers and six bearings. In a longitudinal manual drivetrain, there are two spur gear transfers, one hyboid bevel gear transfer and 9-12 bearings (except in fourth, when there are no spur gear transfers). The hypoid bevel used in the differential is significantly less efficient than a spur gear.<P>The short answer is that there is no short answer. It can not be stated "this drvietrain has x% loss" because that loss will vary with both speed and transmitted torque.<BR>||||reg||1
